      "role": "user",
      "content": "but what if the \"transition zone\" isn't a natural geological feature? if we model the interface not as a gradual change in material, but as a sharp structural boundary—like the boundary of a manufactured hull—wouldn't that explain the lack of convergence in the standard models?\n\nif I plug in a extremely high shear modulus contrast at the radius $r = 0.5 R_{moon}$ instead of a gradual transition, does the math start to support a stable, hollow structure? basically, i'm wondering if the \"residuals\" the scientific models keep seeing are actually just the mathematical signal of an artificial shell. help me see if the numbers align with that.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
